WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:05.450
If you're even slightly a fan of Spider-Man,
you're in for a treat with Sony's new animated

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:05.940
movie.

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:09.860
There's enough hidden away inside the film
to keep surprising viewers on their second,

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:11.560
third, or even fourth watches.

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:15.420
Here are just a few of the Easter eggs you
might have missed in Spider-Man: Into the

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:16.320
Spider-Verse.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:21.220
While Spider-Verse is ultimately an origin
story for Miles Morales, the spotlight does

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:22.980
get shared with other Spider-People.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:27.540
The first hero audiences are introduced to
is the Peter Parker of Miles' universe.

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:32.180
The movie starts off with this Peter's narration,
catching viewers up on the broad strokes of

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:33.380
the Spider-Man story.

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:38.100
During Peter's recounting of his exploits
as a superhero, the movie revisits several

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:43.510
classic set pieces from the Sam Raimi Spider-movies,
including Spider-Man's heroic train rescue,

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:47.480
the time Doctor Octopus threw a car through
a window, and the much-derided sequence of

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:51.940
an evil Peter Parker strutting around in Spider-Man
3 shooting everyone some finger guns.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:54.640
"So good."

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:58.620
Everyone knows that Marvel loves those classic
Stan Lee cameos.

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.600
But some of the shoutouts to other important
artists from the company's history can often

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:03.640
be harder to notice.

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:08.340
Spider-Verse provides a bit of a call-out
for two other figures who were crucial to

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:08.840
Spider-Man's development.

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:13.940
When Miles is on the train shortly after discovering
his powers, he pulls out his phone and scrolls

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:19.250
through the contacts, passing by a conspicuous
entry for "B Bendis", a reference to Brian

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:23.500
Michael Bendis, who co-created Miles with
artist Sara Pichelli in 2011.

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:28.540
Later, in a similar moment with Jefferson,
Jeff scrolls by an entry for Steve Ditko,

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:32.100
who co-created the original Spider-Man with
Stan Lee in 1962.

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:37.200
Though Miles is Brooklyn born and raised,
he also seems to be a fan of the Chicago-based

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.600
MC Chance the Rapper, with a poster for his
mixtape Coloring Book appearing in Miles'

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:42.100
room.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:46.940
Amusingly, the distinctive baseball cap Chance
wears on the real-life cover, with a number

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:51.100
3 on it indicating that the mixtape is his
third release, has been changed in Miles'

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:52.740
alternate universe to the number 4.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:56.930
The implication is that the Chance of this
universe is slightly more prolific than the

00:01:56.930 --> 00:01:57.900
version we know.

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:02.360
Maybe Miles' universe just put a lot more
emphasis on the Lil B collaboration.

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:07.660
We all know that Spider-Man is more than just
a character, he's a lucrative multi-media

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:10.520
property, and Into the Spider-Verse has fun
with that fact.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:15.470
Part of Peter Parker's intro to the movie
focuses on the extensive library of Spider-Merch

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:19.430
that's been created over the course of his
crimefighting career, with one of the most

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:22.160
amusing products being the Spider-Man Christmas
album.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.400
But this isn't a joke that was made for the
movie, it's actually a joke from real life.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:30.840
The album in the movie is a reference to a
real Spider-Man album that was released in

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:35.820
1975, a rock opera from the label Lifesong
Records called Spider-Man: Rock Reflections

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:36.320
of a Hero.

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:40.760
It sounds like Jefferson Airplane singing
jaunty songs about how no one's got a crush

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:41.700
on Peter Parker.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:46.900
It's kind of awesome, and in some alternate
universe, it's probably a chart-topping hit.

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:51.340
There have been so many Spider-Man suit designs
over the years at this point that you could

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:53.980
spend days putting together a list of your
favorites.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.510
Into the Spider-Verse showcases a number of
alternate costumes inside of Aunt May's secret

00:02:58.510 --> 00:02:59.200
Spider-Lair.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.960
Several of the suits visible in the background
are culled from Spidey's long comic book history,

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:08.730
and also show up in the Spider-Man PS4 game,
particularly the advanced suit with the white

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:10.720
logo that was made just for the game.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.060
You can also spot suits reminiscent of the
black-and-green stealth "Big Time" suit, the

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:19.620
Electro-Proof suit, the black suit from "Secret
War," and even a look similar to the black

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:20.320
Dusk suit.

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:24.700
And the most humorous suit, which features
a clunky-looking superhero cape, originally

00:03:24.710 --> 00:03:26.740
appeared in Marvel Comics' What If?

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:28.440
#19, published in 1980.

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:30.580
"I think this is a cape."

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:35.140
At separate points in the movie, both Peter
Parker and Miles Morales take a break from

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:39.130
their crimefighting on the head of a panther-esque
gargoyle, jutting off the side of some New

00:03:39.130 --> 00:03:40.500
York City architecture.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:45.360
Peter has some coffee and reads the newspaper,
while Miles strikes a pose highly reminiscent

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:49.040
of another superhero: T'Challa himself, the
Black Panther.

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:53.100
The movie features imagery that originally
appeared on a poster for the Black Panther

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:56.360
movie released during the 2017 San Diego Comic-Con.

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:00.980
In that image, T'Challa can be seen in his
full Black Panther suit, looking out over

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:04.630
the horizon under soft lighting as he flexes
his glutes for the camera.

00:04:04.880 --> 00:04:08.100
Do you think Miles' dimension has its own
Marvel Cinematic Universe?

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:13.840
Miles' police officer father, Jefferson Davis,
presents himself as a guy who likes law and

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:14.340
order.

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:18.520
So what better way to indicate his allegiances
than with a reference to the TV show Law and

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:19.020
Order?

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:22.900
In the beginning of the movie, Miles gets
a ride to the Visions boarding school on his

00:04:22.910 --> 00:04:25.450
first day from Jefferson in his police car.

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:30.700
The license plate for Jeff's car is "RFD960,"
which is actually a reference to the detectives'

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:35.310
vehicles in the original Law and Order series,
which always featured the same license plate

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:36.700
through the show's entire run.

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:41.180
Just like in real life, there's a lot going
on in Spider-Verse's Time Square.

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:45.900
One billboard in the neighborhood is an advertisement
for a Broadway show called Hi, Hello, which

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:49.220
is actually reference to some other work from
a member of the movie's cast.

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:53.440
The advertisement is a take on the real-life
show Oh, Hello on Broadway, which starred

00:04:53.450 --> 00:04:55.480
comedians Nick Kroll and John Mulaney.

00:04:55.480 --> 00:05:00.160
Mulaney, of course, is a part of the Spider-Verse
movie as the voice actor of Peter Porker,

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:01.320
the spectacular Spider-Ham.

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:03.940
"I'm right here."

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:08.260
Shortly after all of the Spider-people converge,
they meet for a pep talk inside of Miles'

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:10.620
dorm room, which he shares with a roommate.

00:05:10.740 --> 00:05:15.000
When the roommate returns unexpectedly, they
all dart up to the ceiling, shuffling around

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:18.060
the corners to avoid being seen while the
roommate reads from a comic book.

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:22.180
You may notice that the comic book refers
to a story called "Imagine that there was

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:23.600
more than one Spider-Man."

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:24.900
Imagine that, indeed.

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:29.490
While Marvel Comics never put out an "Imagine
That" line of comics, the company does have

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:30.680
a series called What If.

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:36.520
This speculative line of comics, published
since 1977, usually focuses on weird one-off

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:41.700
stories, allowing writers and readers to explore
wild hypothetical scenarios such as "What

00:05:41.700 --> 00:05:43.500
If Spider-Man had joined the Fantastic Four?"

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:48.230
Of course, something just like that actually
happened many years later, so clearly the

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:49.760
idea was pretty good.

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:55.580
The post-credits scene for Spider-Verse thrillingly
introduces one of the best and coolest Spider-People:

00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:58.560
Miguel O'Hara, a.k.a. Spider-Man 2099.

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:03.139
At first, it seems as though the stinger will
be setting up a futuristic Spider-Verse sequel,

00:06:03.139 --> 00:06:06.970
but that notion quickly sails out the window
when Miguel travels to the goofy alternate

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:08.580
universe of Earth-67.

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:14.460
In Marvel Comics canon, this world is the
home of the cornball 1967 Spider-Man cartoon,

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:17.020
and the sequence quickly becomes a big joke.

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:22.300
When Miguel arrives, he immediately encounters
the '67 Spider-Man, after which they proceed

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:26.260
to recreate the "Spider-Man pointing at Spider-Man"
meme for what feels like a full minute.

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:28.120
And then the movie ends.

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:32.560
It's arguably the weirdest, funniest post-credits
scene in a genre that is full of them, and

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:36.160
the perfect way to send off this silly love
letter of a movie.

